# pnpm: Present as build tool in Docker image (corepack), not used at runtime.
# Image ships pnpm@10.34.5 (installed via corepack prepare in the Dockerfiles).
CVE-2025-69262
CVE-2025-69263

# pnpm: Repository-controlled configDependencies can select a malicious native
# install engine → arbitrary code execution (CVE-2026-55697 / GHSA-gj8w-mvpf-x27x).
# Fixed only in the pnpm 11.x line (>=11.5.3); NO backport to the 10.x line, and
# bumping the monorepo to pnpm 11 would disturb the pnpm@10-specific Prisma hoisting
# in apps/api/Dockerfile (runtime stage). Not exploitable in our image: the exploit
# requires `pnpm install` to process attacker-controlled configDependencies. The
# production entrypoint never runs `pnpm install` — it uses the Prisma CLI + Node
# directly, and the only pnpm invocation is `pnpm tsx` (a run command) against our
# own baked, trusted workspace. Altering configDependencies would require write
# access to the image filesystem (= full compromise already). The other 7 CVEs in
# this pnpm cluster ARE fixed by the 10.33.0 → 10.34.5 bump. Revisit when a 10.x
# backport lands or when the monorepo moves to pnpm 11.
CVE-2026-55697

# picomatch ReDoS in npm's bundled tinyglobby (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/...).
# Not exploitable: npm is a build/dev tool in the image, never exposed to user input at runtime.
# App code already uses picomatch@4.0.4 (pnpm lockfile). Revisit when npm bundles tinyglobby
# with picomatch>=4.0.4 internally.
CVE-2026-33671

# fast-jwt: Cache Confusion via cacheKeyBuilder Collisions (CVE-2026-35039).
# No fix available as of 2026-04-09. Risk is low: JWT tokens are short-lived access tokens
# (not cache keys used across tenants). cacheKeyBuilder is used internally by fast-jwt for
# algorithm validation; not exposed to user-controlled input. Revisit when fast-jwt >6.2.1
# releases a fix.
CVE-2026-35039

# fast-jwt: Accepts unknown `crit` header extensions (RFC 7515 §4.1.11) (CVE-2026-35042).
# No fix available — package marked as "affected". Risk is low in our setup: JWTs are
# issued exclusively by our API with a fixed algorithm (HS256/RS256 via JWT_SECRET).
# No external JWTs or untrusted issuers are accepted. Revisit when fast-jwt releases a fix.
CVE-2026-35042

# node-tar: DoS via crafted gzip bomb / malformed tar archive (CVE-2026-59873 CRIT / -59874).
# Present ONLY in build tooling bundles, never in the runtime app dependency tree:
#   /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/tar (npm's own bundled tar) and
#   corepack's pnpm cache (…/corepack/v1/pnpm/*/dist/node_modules/tar).
# npm@latest (11.12.x) still bundles the vulnerable tar 7.5.11 — no fix flows via `npm i -g
# npm@latest` yet. Not exploitable: npm/pnpm run at build time against our own trusted,
# baked workspace; the production entrypoint (node + prisma CLI + `pnpm tsx`) never extracts
# untrusted archives. Revisit when npm/corepack bundle tar>=7.5.19.
CVE-2026-59873
CVE-2026-59874

# sigstore: Unauthorized certificates accepted (CVE-2026-48815). Present ONLY in npm's bundle
# (/usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/sigstore), used by `npm audit signatures` /
# provenance verification at build/dev time — never in the runtime app tree or entrypoint.
# npm@latest still bundles the affected sigstore. Revisit when npm bundles sigstore>=4.1.1.
CVE-2026-48815

# brace-expansion: ReDoS (CVE-2026-14257 / -13149) + unbounded-intermediate-arrays DoS
# (CVE-2026-69152). The vulnerable copies (2.1.2 / 5.0.4) live ONLY in npm's bundle and
# corepack's pnpm cache (build tooling). Our app dependency tree resolves brace-expansion@5.0.9
# (fixed for all three, via the pnpm.overrides pin bumped 5.0.8 -> 5.0.9 for CVE-2026-69152), so
# no runtime code path is affected. Revisit when npm/corepack bundle brace-expansion>=5.0.9.
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-13149
CVE-2026-69152
